<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis XIII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1601–1643</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis XIII, son of Henry IV, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1610. In 1620 he united Béarn and Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> with the French crown and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> there. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> as chief minister from 1624 he took the Huguenot stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> in 1628 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1635, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> in 1640. In 1642 the prince of Sedan ceded his principality to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis XIII of France" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe de Champaigne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis_XIII,_rey_de_Francia_(Philippe_de_Champaigne).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
